1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:13,996
On the last morning of January, nineteen twenty one, a lookout at the Cape Hatteras Coast Guard station looked out to sea and saw a five masted schooner sitting on the most dangerous sandbank on the American coast.

2
00:00:14,316 --> 00:00:16,110
Every sail was still set.

3
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:19,841
She was hard aground, and the surf was breaking over her.

4
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:23,041
It took four days for anyone to reach her.

5
00:00:23,361 --> 00:00:26,646
When they finally climbed aboard, there was no one there.

6
00:00:26,966 --> 00:00:28,956
Not the captain, not the crew.

7
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:33,669
In the galley, food looked as if it had been prepared for the next day's meal.

8
00:00:34,369 --> 00:00:36,166
Eleven men had vanished.

9
00:00:36,486 --> 00:00:42,653
Five departments of the United States government would spend almost two years trying to find out why.

10
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,395
Her name was the Carroll A.

11
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:47,823
Deering.

12
00:00:48,143 --> 00:00:54,764
Today she is called the ghost ship of the Outer Banks, and like most ghost stories, hers has grown in the telling.

13
00:00:55,084 --> 00:00:57,623
So tonight we will tell it from the record.

14
00:00:57,943 --> 00:01:03,705
Who was on that ship, what they said, who saw them last, and what the boarding party actually found.

15
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:08,647
At the end, we will separate what is documented from what has been added since.

16
00:01:10,497 --> 00:01:11,865
Start with the ship.

17
00:01:12,185 --> 00:01:13,448
The Carroll A.

18
00:01:13,768 --> 00:01:21,837
Deering was built in nineteen nineteen at the Deering yard in Bath, Maine, one of the last great wooden sailing ships of the East Coast.

19
00:01:22,157 --> 00:01:29,218
She was two hundred and fifty five feet long, with five masts, and she was built to carry bulk cargo like coal.

20
00:01:29,538 --> 00:01:31,806
The owner named her after his son.

21
00:01:32,506 --> 00:01:36,889
By then, schooners like her were already becoming a thing of the past.

22
00:01:37,209 --> 00:01:38,984
Steam was taking over.

23
00:01:39,304 --> 00:01:46,411
But a big schooner was cheap to run, needed a small crew, and could still make money hauling coal to South America.

24
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:50,884
That is exactly what she was doing in the summer of nineteen twenty.

25
00:01:52,734 --> 00:01:58,155
In late August, the Deering left Virginia for Rio de Janeiro with a load of coal.

26
00:01:58,475 --> 00:02:07,293
Her captain was William Merritt, a respected officer who had survived the sinking of his previous ship by a German submarine in the war.

27
00:02:07,613 --> 00:02:10,272
His son Sewall sailed as first mate.

28
00:02:10,972 --> 00:02:14,335
A few days out, Captain Merritt fell seriously ill.

29
00:02:14,655 --> 00:02:19,106
The ship turned back and put him and his son ashore at Lewes, Delaware.

30
00:02:19,425 --> 00:02:27,817
The owners needed a replacement quickly, and they found one in Willis Wormell, a sixty six year old captain who had come out of retirement.

31
00:02:28,137 --> 00:02:31,197
With him came a new first mate, Charles McLellan.

32
00:02:31,897 --> 00:02:35,035
On the eighth of September, the Deering sailed again.

33
00:02:35,355 --> 00:02:42,637
She reached Rio, delivered her coal, and on the second of December started home, stopping at Barbados for supplies.

34
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:47,903
It was in Barbados that the story starts to turn.

35
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:53,275
According to the testimony gathered later, McLellan went ashore and got drunk.

36
00:02:53,595 --> 00:02:58,707
He complained to another captain, Hugh Norton of the schooner Augustus W.

37
00:02:59,027 --> 00:03:04,214
Snow, that he could not discipline the crew because Captain Wormell kept interfering.

38
00:03:04,534 --> 00:03:09,537
He said he was doing the navigating himself because the captain's eyesight was poor.

39
00:03:10,237 --> 00:03:13,733
And then, according to the people who heard him, he said this.

40
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:17,158
I'll get the captain before we get to Norfolk.

41
00:03:17,478 --> 00:03:18,571
I will.

42
00:03:19,271 --> 00:03:21,862
McLellan was arrested for being drunk.

43
00:03:22,182 --> 00:03:25,303
And here is the part that is hard to understand.

44
00:03:25,623 --> 00:03:27,548
Captain Wormell forgave him.

45
00:03:27,868 --> 00:03:39,622
He paid his bail, took him back aboard, and on the ninth of January the Deering left Barbados for Hampton Roads, Virginia, with the same first mate who had just been heard threatening him.

46
00:03:41,472 --> 00:03:44,320
For almost three weeks, nobody saw the Deering.

47
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:50,382
Then, at the end of January, she came past the Cape Lookout lightship off North Carolina.

48
00:03:50,702 --> 00:03:54,657
Accounts differ on whether this was the twenty eighth or the twenty ninth.

49
00:03:55,357 --> 00:04:03,186
The lightship's keeper, Captain Thomas Jacobson, watched her come close and heard a man on her deck call over through a megaphone.

50
00:04:03,506 --> 00:04:09,852
The man said the schooner had lost her anchors in a storm off Cape Fear, and asked that the owners be told.

51
00:04:10,552 --> 00:04:13,143
Jacobson could not pass the message on.

52
00:04:13,463 --> 00:04:15,542
His radio was not working.

53
00:04:15,862 --> 00:04:17,402
But he noticed things.

54
00:04:17,722 --> 00:04:26,648
The man who spoke to him was tall and thin, with reddish hair and a foreign accent, and Jacobson did not think he behaved like an officer.

55
00:04:26,968 --> 00:04:34,968
The rest of the crew were milling around on the quarterdeck, at the back of the ship, a place ordinary sailors were normally kept away from.

56
00:04:35,668 --> 00:04:37,784
Captain Wormell was sixty six.

57
00:04:38,104 --> 00:04:41,924
Nobody described him as tall, red haired, or foreign.

58
00:04:43,774 --> 00:04:49,477
The next day, another vessel reported a schooner on a course heading straight for Diamond Shoals.

59
00:04:49,797 --> 00:04:51,793
Her crew saw no one on deck.

60
00:04:52,113 --> 00:04:53,601
They did not hail her.

61
00:04:54,301 --> 00:04:57,298
That is the last time anyone saw the Carroll A.

62
00:04:57,618 --> 00:05:00,019
Deering under sail with anyone aboard.

63
00:05:01,869 --> 00:05:06,007
To understand what happened next, you need to know where she was heading.

64
00:05:06,327 --> 00:05:12,658
Diamond Shoals is a field of shifting sandbanks that reaches miles out to sea from Cape Hatteras.

65
00:05:12,978 --> 00:05:21,403
The warm Gulf Stream and the cold Labrador Current meet here, and the storms that form on the boundary are some of the worst on the coast.

66
00:05:21,723 --> 00:05:25,110
Sailors call this stretch the Graveyard of the Atlantic.

67
00:05:25,430 --> 00:05:27,708
Hundreds of ships have been wrecked on it.

68
00:05:28,408 --> 00:05:33,725
A competent crew does not sail a ship onto Diamond Shoals with every sail set.

69
00:05:34,045 --> 00:05:35,400
Not on purpose.

70
00:05:37,250 --> 00:05:40,311
On the thirty first of January, surfman C.

71
00:05:40,631 --> 00:05:41,597
P.

72
00:05:41,917 --> 00:05:47,590
Brady at the Cape Hatteras station saw her at first light, stuck on the outer edge of the shoals.

73
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:51,906
The weather was bad, and for days no rescue boat could get close.

74
00:05:52,606 --> 00:05:56,516
On the fourth of February, a salvage crew finally boarded her.

75
00:05:58,366 --> 00:06:00,366
Here is what they reported.

76
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:02,847
The ship's wheel was smashed.

77
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:06,896
The binnacle, the stand that holds the compass, had been broken.

78
00:06:07,216 --> 00:06:09,541
The rudder had come loose from its fitting.

79
00:06:09,861 --> 00:06:11,842
The ship could not have been steered.

80
00:06:12,542 --> 00:06:14,296
The ship's log was gone.

81
00:06:14,616 --> 00:06:16,995
The navigation instruments were gone.

82
00:06:17,315 --> 00:06:19,840
The crew's personal belongings were gone.

83
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:22,157
And both lifeboats were gone.

84
00:06:22,857 --> 00:06:26,750
In the galley, food appeared to have been prepared for the next day's meal.

85
00:06:27,070 --> 00:06:32,430
Later retellings would turn that into a pot of soup and coffee left warm on the stove.

86
00:06:33,130 --> 00:06:34,978
There was no sign of a fight.

87
00:06:35,298 --> 00:06:36,538
No blood.

88
00:06:36,858 --> 00:06:38,235
No bodies.

89
00:06:38,555 --> 00:06:45,471
Just a ship with her sails up and her people missing, and whatever was in those lifeboats, which was never seen again.

90
00:06:47,321 --> 00:06:49,896
Think about what that list actually tells us.

91
00:06:50,216 --> 00:06:54,113
Somebody took the log, the instruments and the personal effects.

92
00:06:54,433 --> 00:06:59,106
Those are the things you take when you leave a ship deliberately, with time to spare.

93
00:06:59,426 --> 00:07:02,164
The lifeboats were launched, not torn away.

94
00:07:02,484 --> 00:07:07,127
Whatever happened was not a sudden disaster where men were thrown overboard.

95
00:07:07,447 --> 00:07:09,373
Someone decided to leave.

96
00:07:10,073 --> 00:07:14,713
But the broken wheel and the smashed compass stand are harder to explain.

97
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:16,393
Storm damage?

98
00:07:16,713 --> 00:07:18,744
The surf, after she grounded?

99
00:07:19,064 --> 00:07:21,254
Or something that happened before?

100
00:07:23,104 --> 00:07:25,047
There was no saving the Deering.

101
00:07:25,367 --> 00:07:28,818
She was stuck fast, and she was a danger to other ships.

102
00:07:29,138 --> 00:07:31,851
In March, the Coast Guard dynamited her.

103
00:07:32,171 --> 00:07:35,773
Pieces of her washed up along the Outer Banks for years.

104
00:07:36,093 --> 00:07:39,594
Some timbers ended up in houses on Hatteras Island.

105
00:07:39,914 --> 00:07:47,116
Her bell and her capstan survived, and you can still see them today at the Graveyard of the Atlantic Museum in Hatteras.

106
00:07:47,816 --> 00:07:49,267
The ship was gone.

107
00:07:49,587 --> 00:07:51,246
The questions were not.

108
00:07:53,096 --> 00:08:00,264
In April, a fisherman named Christopher Gray said he had found a bottle washed up near Buxton, on Hatteras Island.

109
00:08:00,584 --> 00:08:02,212
Inside was a note.

110
00:08:02,532 --> 00:08:08,403
It said the Deering had been captured by an oil burning boat, and that the crew were being held and hiding.

111
00:08:09,103 --> 00:08:11,551
For a while, this looked like the answer.

112
00:08:11,871 --> 00:08:16,139
Captain Wormell's widow believed the handwriting matched one of the crew.

113
00:08:17,989 --> 00:08:19,286
It was a fake.

114
00:08:19,606 --> 00:08:24,127
Government handwriting experts concluded that Gray had written it himself.

115
00:08:24,447 --> 00:08:26,697
Under questioning, he admitted it.

116
00:08:27,017 --> 00:08:31,637
He had hoped the attention would help him get a job at the Cape Hatteras lighthouse.

117
00:08:32,337 --> 00:08:38,074
It was the one piece of evidence that pointed clearly at a crime, and it turned out to be invented.

118
00:08:39,924 --> 00:08:46,385
Still, the federal government took the case seriously, and that is part of what makes this story unusual.

119
00:08:46,705 --> 00:08:52,759
Five departments were involved: Commerce, Treasury, Justice, the Navy, and the State Department.

120
00:08:53,079 --> 00:08:59,532
The Secretary of Commerce at the time was Herbert Hoover, the future president, and he had a reason to care.

121
00:08:59,852 --> 00:09:04,603
The Deering was not the only ship to disappear off that coast in those months.

122
00:09:04,923 --> 00:09:11,885
A steamship called the Hewitt, carrying sulfur, vanished in the same area at about the same time, with all hands.

123
00:09:12,585 --> 00:09:18,131
Investigators read lightship logs, interviewed witnesses, and chased every theory.

124
00:09:18,451 --> 00:09:21,565
In late nineteen twenty two, the inquiry closed.

125
00:09:21,885 --> 00:09:23,586
No official finding.

126
00:09:25,436 --> 00:09:27,385
So what are the explanations?

127
00:09:28,085 --> 00:09:29,328
Hurricane.

128
00:09:29,648 --> 00:09:33,995
The Weather Bureau favored storms, and the area is notorious for them.

129
00:09:34,315 --> 00:09:40,830
But the Deering's crew had time to take the log and their belongings, which does not look like a panicked escape.

130
00:09:41,530 --> 00:09:42,655
Pirates.

131
00:09:42,975 --> 00:09:46,936
Some officials believed it, and so did Captain Wormell's widow.

132
00:09:47,256 --> 00:09:51,724
But no one ever found a pirate ship, a prisoner, or a piece of cargo.

133
00:09:52,424 --> 00:09:54,468
Communist revolutionaries.

134
00:09:54,788 --> 00:09:58,042
In nineteen twenty one, this was taken seriously.

135
00:09:58,362 --> 00:10:02,635
Papers seized in New York reportedly urged the seizure of ships.

136
00:10:02,955 --> 00:10:04,898
Nothing linked them to the Deering.

137
00:10:05,598 --> 00:10:06,946
Rum runners.

138
00:10:07,266 --> 00:10:11,738
Prohibition had just begun, and smugglers were active along the coast.

139
00:10:12,058 --> 00:10:18,139
But a huge, slow, five masted schooner is about the worst ship imaginable for smuggling.

140
00:10:18,839 --> 00:10:20,056
And mutiny.

141
00:10:20,376 --> 00:10:23,897
A senator from Maine called it a plain case of mutiny.

142
00:10:25,747 --> 00:10:27,627
Look at what we actually have.

143
00:10:27,947 --> 00:10:31,714
A first mate heard threatening to get the captain before Norfolk.

144
00:10:32,034 --> 00:10:36,525
A captain who, according to his mate, could not see well enough to navigate.

145
00:10:36,845 --> 00:10:44,591
A tall, red haired, foreign stranger speaking for the ship at the lightship, with the crew gathered where they were not supposed to be.

146
00:10:44,911 --> 00:10:49,873
And then a ship abandoned in an orderly way, with the log and instruments taken.

147
00:10:50,573 --> 00:10:52,517
None of that proves a mutiny.

148
00:10:52,837 --> 00:10:58,538
But it is the explanation that uses the most of the evidence, and it has to ignore the least.

149
00:11:00,388 --> 00:11:02,198
So here is where we land.

150
00:11:02,898 --> 00:11:09,226
Documented: the Deering left Barbados in January with a first mate who had threatened the captain.

151
00:11:09,546 --> 00:11:15,638
A red haired man who did not seem to be an officer spoke for the ship at the Cape Lookout lightship.

152
00:11:15,958 --> 00:11:18,960
She grounded on Diamond Shoals with sails set.

153
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:28,605
The wheel and compass stand were wrecked, the log, instruments, belongings and both lifeboats were gone, and food appeared to have been prepared in the galley.

154
00:11:28,925 --> 00:11:31,004
The bottle message was a hoax.

155
00:11:31,324 --> 00:11:34,262
The investigation closed without a finding.

156
00:11:34,962 --> 00:11:37,802
Legend: the exact menu on the stove.

157
00:11:38,122 --> 00:11:40,450
The cat that supposedly survived.

158
00:11:40,770 --> 00:11:45,657
And the idea that the ship was swallowed by something supernatural off Hatteras.

159
00:11:47,507 --> 00:11:50,352
Eleven men left Barbados on that ship.

160
00:11:50,672 --> 00:11:55,859
None of them was ever seen again, not in a lifeboat, not in a port, not in a court.

161
00:11:56,179 --> 00:12:01,478
If there was a mutiny, the mutineers vanished as completely as the men they turned on.

162
00:12:02,178 --> 00:12:04,502
So I will ask you what I asked myself.

163
00:12:04,822 --> 00:12:11,418
If you had been the investigator in nineteen twenty one, with that list in front of you, what would you have concluded?

164
00:12:11,738 --> 00:12:13,218
Tell me in the comments.

165
00:12:15,068 --> 00:12:17,996
The sources for this story are in the description.

166
00:12:18,316 --> 00:12:24,490
If you like true stories that history never finished, subscribe, and I will see you in the next one.

167
00:12:24,810 --> 00:12:26,772
This is The Quiet Archive.
